{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012nwmd22vXmGjpXM55Bhgvo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tScrape ID: 01a14720-fc70-76ae-b92f-b778735801af\n2\tYou can set the environment variable `__VITE_ADDITIONAL_SERVER_ALLOWED_HOSTS` to add additional allowed hosts. Use commas to separate multiple hosts (e.g., `host1.example.com,host2.example.com`).\n3\t\n4\t## server.port [​](https://vite.dev/config/server-options\\#server-port)\n5\t\n6\t- **Type:**`number`\n7\t- **Default:**`5173`\n8\t\n9\tSpecify server port. Note if the port is already being used, Vite will automatically try the next available port so this may not be the actual port the server ends up listening on.\n10\t\n11\t## server.strictPort [​](https://vite.dev/config/server-options\\#server-strictport)\n12\t\n13\t- **Type:**`boolean`\n14\t\n15\tSet to `true` to exit if port is already in use, instead of automatically trying the next available port.\n16\t--\n17\t- `protocol` \\- WebSocket protocol (`ws` or `wss`)\n18\t- `host` \\- WebSocket server host\n19\t- `port` \\- WebSocket server port\n20\t- `path` \\- WebSocket path\n21\t- `clientPort` \\- Override the port on the client side, allowing you to serve the websocket on a different port than the client code looks for it on\n22\t- `timeout` \\- Connection timeout in milliseconds (default: 30000)\n23\t- `server` \\- Use a custom HTTP server for WebSocket connections\n24\t\n25\tWhen `server.ws.server` is defined, Vite will process the WebSocket connection requests through the provided server. If not in middleware mode, Vite will attempt to process WebSocket connection requests through the existing server. This can be helpful when using self-signed certificates or when you want to expose Vite over a network on a single port.\n26\t--\n27\t\n28\t- configure the reverse proxy to proxy WebSocket too\n29\t- set [`server.strictPort = true`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-strictport) and set `server.ws.clientPort` to the same value with `server.port`\n30\t- set `server.ws.port` to a different value from [`server.port`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-port)\n31\t\n32\t## server.forwardConsole [​](https://vite.dev/config/server-options\\#server-forwardconsole)\n33\t\n34\t- **Type:**`boolean | { unhandledErrors?: boolean, logLevels?: ('error' | 'warn' | 'info' | 'log' | 'debug')[] }`\n35\t- **Default:** auto (`true` when an AI coding agent is detected based on [`@vercel/detect-agent`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@vercel/detect-agent), otherwise `false`)\n36\t\n37\t\n38\t[exited with code 0]\n39\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d8c63bf-831a-42e1-b2ac-70fe72e19365","uuid":"b7f21763-44f3-43f7-b134-e6725fa4201d","timestamp":"2026-10-16T23:51:48.699Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-0/-tmp-fceval-t06-t2-wbij8l1x-work/7d8c63bf-831a-42e1-b2ac-70fe72e19365/tasks/bab5lwi4t.output","content":"Scrape ID: 01a14720-fc70-76ae-b92f-b778735801af\nYou can set the environment variable `__VITE_ADDITIONAL_SERVER_ALLOWED_HOSTS` to add additional allowed hosts. Use commas to separate multiple hosts (e.g., `host1.example.com,host2.example.com`).\n\n## server.port [​](https://vite.dev/config/server-options\\#server-port)\n\n- **Type:**`number`\n- **Default:**`5173`\n\nSpecify server port. Note if the port is already being used, Vite will automatically try the next available port so this may not be the actual port the server ends up listening on.\n\n## server.strictPort [​](https://vite.dev/config/server-options\\#server-strictport)\n\n- **Type:**`boolean`\n\nSet to `true` to exit if port is already in use, instead of automatically trying the next available port.\n--\n- `protocol` \\- WebSocket protocol (`ws` or `wss`)\n- `host` \\- WebSocket server host\n- `port` \\- WebSocket server port\n- `path` \\- WebSocket path\n- `clientPort` \\- Override the port on the client side, allowing you to serve the websocket on a different port than the client code looks for it on\n- `timeout` \\- Connection timeout in milliseconds (default: 30000)\n- `server` \\- Use a custom HTTP server for WebSocket connections\n\nWhen `server.ws.server` is defined, Vite will process the WebSocket connection requests through the provided server. If not in middleware mode, Vite will attempt to process WebSocket connection requests through the existing server. This can be helpful when using self-signed certificates or when you want to expose Vite over a network on a single port.\n--\n\n- configure the reverse proxy to proxy WebSocket too\n- set [`server.strictPort = true`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-strictport) and set `server.ws.clientPort` to the same value with `server.port`\n- set `server.ws.port` to a different value from [`server.port`](https://vite.dev/config/server-options#server-port)\n\n## server.forwardConsole [​](https://vite.dev/config/server-options\\#server-forwardconsole)\n\n- **Type:**`boolean | { unhandledErrors?: boolean, logLevels?: ('error' | 'warn' | 'info' | 'log' | 'debug')[] }`\n- **Default:** auto (`true` when an AI coding agent is detected based on [`@vercel/detect-agent`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@vercel/detect-agent), otherwise `false`)\n\n\n[exited with code 0]\n","numLines":39,"startLine":1,"totalLines":39}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012nwmd22vXmGjpXM55Bhgvo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
